---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/expert/anthropology-of-the-contemporary">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Anthropology of the contemporary turns the discipline's tools on <strong>the modern
    world itself</strong> — labs, markets, algorithms, bureaucracies, crises. The wager: the
    ethnographic gaze that revealed other societies' logic reveals OUR institutions' logic just
    as well, including the parts they don't know about themselves.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="studying up, inside machines, inside crises" widget="equationStep" config={contempCfg} />

  <h2>The move in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Financial anthropology:</strong> Ho's "Liquidated" (2009) — Wall Street's culture of expendability explained behaviour the spreadsheets couldn't; Riles on the documents and models finance lives inside.</li>
    <li><strong>Infrastructure and standards:</strong> Star: infrastructure is invisible until it breaks — studying standards bodies, classifications and plumbing (literal and organisational) shows how the modern world actually holds together.</li>
    <li><strong>Applied and public anthropology:</strong> product teams, public health campaigns, humanitarian NGOs hire ethnographers for exactly this: interventions fail when they ignore local logics (the development lesson's RCT era converged on the same conclusion).</li>
    <li><strong>Why it completes the atlas:</strong> every ladder in this atlas describes a system; this lesson is the discipline that asks how systems feel from inside — the human sciences' closing move.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nader, "Studying Up" (1969); Ho, Liquidated (2009); Traweek, Beamtimes and Lifetimes (1988); Star, "The Ethnography of Infrastructure" (1999); Seaver on algorithms.</p>
</TopicPage>
